The "align with live API" refactors updated Result::Item and the fixtures but not their consumers, so two offline test files fail (they no longer hang after the karr #4 MockUA fix, they fail fast):

t/19-arcraiders-requirements.t (exit 255): calculate_requirements / calculate_base_requirements / find_item_by_* rely on synthetic items (Ferro I/II/III, Metal Parts, Steel Spring, Advanced Circuit, Basic Circuit) that commit 78c6723 removed when items.json was replaced with the real live /items response (20 real items, none of them craftable). Worse, commit ab47840 dropped the crafting_requirements attribute (was mapped from $data->{components}) from Result::Item, but ArcRaiders.pm still calls $item->crafting_requirements at lines 770 and 854. The live /items endpoint returns no crafting/components data at all, so the requirements feature has no live data source. _ensure_items_cache passes includeComponents=>true, suggesting components may come from that variant, but there is no fixture for it and t/ is offline.

t/30-cli.t (exit 255): dies at CLI/Cmd/Items.pm line 93 calling $item->category (removed from Result::Item). CLI/Cmd/Item.pm also calls removed accessors slug/category/stats/base_value/crafting_requirements/sold_by/recycle_yield/last_updated, and t/30 asserts synthetic Ferro/metal-parts data no longer in the fixture. This is the "dropping a Result attribute breaks the CLI silently at the source level" hazard.

Fix needs a maintainer decision (out of scope for karr #2/#3/#4): either re-plumb crafting/components from a real live source (e.g. includeComponents) and realign CLI/Cmd/Item.pm + Items.pm + t/19 + t/30 to the live Result::Item shape, or drop the requirements feature and the stale CLI accessors. Re-adding synthetic fixtures is not acceptable (exact-format rule).

Also: t/30 line 8 use Capture::Tiny runs before the BEGIN skip_all guard (line 10), so a missing Capture::Tiny is a hard error not a skip (moot where Capture::Tiny is installed).

Related dependency gap (GitHub issue #1): LWP::Protocol::https is required at runtime for the https://metaforge.app calls but is not declared in cpanfile or dist.ini. It is installed locally (v6.17) so it works here, but a clean install would fail HTTPS. Not fixed here per instruction; do not touch the GitHub issue.

